  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the crank?",
      "a": "The crank is an arm fixed to a rotating shaft, with a pin at its end. As the shaft turns, the pin moves in a circle and drives the connecting rod.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a crank do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the connecting rod do?",
      "a": "The connecting rod links the crank pin to the slider. It changes the circular motion of the pin into the straight to-and-fro motion of the slider, or the other way round.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a conrod?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How far does the slider move?",
      "a": "The slider moves a total distance, called the stroke, of twice the crank length. A crank pin 5 centimetres from the axle gives a 10 centimetre stroke.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the stroke?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a piston turn a wheel?",
      "a": "In an engine, burning fuel pushes the piston, which is the slider. The connecting rod pushes the crank pin round, so the crankshaft turns and drives the wheels.",
      "asks": [
        "How do engines make wheels turn?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are cranks and sliders used?",
      "a": "Car engines, steam locomotives, pumps, sewing machines, jigsaws and some toys all use a crank and slider.",
      "asks": [
        "What uses a crank and slider?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The stroke of a crank and slider is twice the length of the crank.",
    "At the two ends of its travel, called dead centres, the slider stops for an instant before changing direction."
  ],
